ewcommand{\equalQ}{\overset{?}{=}}  egin{array}{c} \work{\varalice}{\varbob} \alicework{\sampleN{t_{1},t_{2}\;}{\varq}}\ \alicework{T = g^{t_{1}}h^{t_{2}}} \alicebob{}{T}{}  obwork{\sampleN{k\;}{\varq}}  obalice{}{k}{} \alicework{s_{1}=x \cdot k+t_{1}}\ \alicework{s_{2}=y \cdot k+t_{2}} \alicebob{}{s_{1},s_{2}}{}  obwork{g^{s_{1}}h^{s_{2}} \equalQ A^{k}T} \end{array} $$ This works because:
$$ g^{s_{1}}h^{s_{2}}=g^{xk+t_{1}}h^{yk+t_{2}}=g^{xk}h^{yk}g^{t_{1}}h^{t_{2}}=\left(g^{x}h^{y}ight)^{k}T=A^{k}T $$ This is secure because, in order to trick Bob, Alice needs to break the discrete log problem.
So, the idea of this ZKP protocol is that Alice is proving that she knows $x$ and $y$ without revealing any of them. For this, Alice computes a commitment and sends it to Bob. Then, Bob will check if the commitment is valid, without the need of knowing $x$ and $y$. Bob only sends a random integer $k$ and checks if the commitment $A$ holds with the value $T$ (chosen by Alice) and $s_1, s_2$ (which depend on $k$).
In this challenge, we are in the position of Alice, we need to convince the server (Bob) that we know the flag&hellip; But we don&rsquo;t!
As it is said, in order to break this ZKP protocol, we need to be able to solve the discrete logarithm problem (DLP), which is not feasible this time because $q = 2 \cdot p + 1$, so it is correctly chosen. Therefore there must be something weird in the protocol implementation.
Finding the flaw The problem is that $k$ is sent to us at the begining, before we send the value $T$:
print(f"Round {_} to convince me, send g^t1 * h^t2 mod q") k = random.randint(2, q-1) print(f"{k = }") T = int(input("&gt;&gt;&gt; ")) As a result, we are able to cheat because we can choose $T$ considering the value of $k$.
Solution The simplest thing is to say that $T = A^{-k}$, so that $A^k \cdot T = 1$. Then we can simply select $s_1 = s_2 = 0$, so that $g^{s_{1}} \cdot h^{s_{2}}= 1$ and the verification holds on Bob&rsquo;s side.
But we need to prove it 5 times, and this approach will only work once because Bob keeps a set of used values for $T$, $s_1$ and $s_2$. However, it is not a problem at all, because we can come up with a general solution:
To simplify, let&rsquo;s choose the value $s$ for both $s_1$ and $s_2$ Then $g^{s_1} \cdot h^{s_2}= g^s \cdot h^s = (gh)^s$ We need that $(gh)^s = A^k \cdot T$ So we can simply set $T = (gh)^s \cdot A^{-k}$ and choose an arbitrary value for $s$ Implementation So, the ZKP protocol is cheated with the following code (I selected $s$ to be the round number, just because):
def get_var(name: str) -&gt; int: io.recvuntil(f'{name} = '.encode()) return int(io.recvline()) io = get_process() q = get_var('q') g = get_var('g') h = get_var('h') A = get_var('A') for r in range(5): k = get_var('k') s1 = s2 = r T = pow(A, -k, q) * pow(g * h, r, q) % q assert pow(g, s1, q) * pow(h, s2, q) % q == T * pow(A, k, q) % q io.sendlineafter(b'&gt;&gt;&gt; ', str(T).encode()) io.sendlineafter(b'&gt;&gt;&gt; ', str(s1).encode()) io.sendlineafter(b'&gt;&gt;&gt; ', str(s2).encode()) io.success(io.recvline().decode()) Flag With all this, we can capture the flag:
$ python3 solve.py 0.cloud.chals.io 34487 [+] Opening connection to 0.cloud.chals.io on port 34487: Done [+] Okay, not bad: HackOn{b4by_1ntr0_t0_z3r0_kn0wl33dg3:)} [*] Closed connection to 0.cloud.chals.io port 34487 The full script can be found in here: solve.py.

